Let $v(x)=2^{x}$, then $2^{u(x)}=v\Bigl(u(x)\Bigr)$, or $(v\circ u)(x)$.

Therefore, the derivative of $2^{u(x)}$ can be found using **the chain rule**:

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\dfrac{d}{dx}2^{u(x)}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{d}{dx}\left[v\Bigl(u(x)\Bigr)\right]&&\gray{\text{Let }v(x)=2^{x}}
\\\\
&=v'\Bigl(u(x)\Bigr)\cdot u'(x)&&\gray{\text{The chain rule}}
\\\\
&=\ln(2)\cdot 2^{u(x)}\cdot u'(x)&&\gray{\dfrac{d}{dx}2^{x}=\ln(2)\cdot 2^{x}}
\end{align}$
